    feat(proc): enforceable private-/proc demand + per-run proc_view attribution (TJ-GAP-088)
    
    The unshare --user jail silently exposes the HOST /proc (~1084 entries,
    /proc/1 = systemd, host cmdlines readable); nothing stopped a deployment
    that assumes containment from getting that view. DECIDED by experiment
    (board row): private procfs is bwrap-only on Ubuntu/AppArmor hosts (the
    mapped-root unshare route is closed: uid_map EPERM), so this tool owns
    the honest boundary, not the primitive.
    
    - New demand, fail closed: --private-proc / TERMINAL_JAIL_PRIVATE_PROC=required
      makes backend selection REFUSE (exit 2, command not run) unless the
      resolved backend can deliver a private procfs — bwrap absent, a failing
      bwrap probe, any unshare launch (pinned/auto/bare), and the composed
      launch (both the ns-failure path and the modify-rewrite path) all refuse
      before running anything. Unknown values are rejected like the other
      control variables. Default (unset) keeps the historical behavior: the
      command runs, nothing refuses.
    - Per-run attribution (criterion 2): every LAUNCH states the delivered
      /proc view on stderr at exec time — proc_view=private (bwrap's fresh
      procfs) / proc_view=host (unshare; the documented known limit) /
      proc_view=platform-owned + proc_view=none (composed launchers). A
      refused run states nothing (nothing was delivered).
    - Standing probe (criterion 3): parity battery cells 1+2 now record
      /proc/1 identity (compared against the host's own init comm) next to
      the entry count; new host-independent cells — 8: the demand enforced
      (FAIL-CLOSED-PROVEN, stub PATH: bwrap absent + unshare probe-failing,
      marker absent) and 9: per-launch attribution (private under bwrap,
      host under unshare, same payload). pytest: plugin/test_private_proc_demand.py
      (20 tests: refusal arms, flag/env equivalence, unknown-value rejection,
      attribution, composed precedence, default-unchanged guard, live
      entry-count + /proc/1 identity cells with HOST-DEGRADED-* skips,
      battery import-and-pin). RED proven two ways: 12/20 fail against the
      pre-change wrapper; the HEAD baseline suite fails exactly those 14
      non-integration tests and nothing else (1548 passed on both sides).
    - Docs (criterion 4): README + docs/backend-parity.md document the
      flag/env, the proc_view vocabulary, and the method note — an
      auto-backend run is NOT evidence about the unshare path (claims about
      unshare require TERMINAL_JAIL_JAIL_BACKEND=unshare); specs/cli.md §4/§6/§7
      updated (demand contract, exit-code table, degradation table).
    
    No regression: full suite plugin/ tests/ -m "not integration" = 1562
    passed, 7 skipped, exit 0 (with the change) vs 1548 passed, 14 failed
    (all fourteen are this task's own new tests against the old wrapper),
    exit 1 on the HEAD baseline; firewall verified live (block verdict still
    exit 126 builtin-rm-rf-root under the demand; the bridge verdict path is
    untouched).
    
    Addresses TJ-GAP-088.
    
    Co-authored-by: Alexis Okuwa <wojonstech@gmail.com>
